 "final_summary": "The bug was caused by a SQL query using `WHERE id >= ?` instead of `WHERE id = ?`, which incorrectly updated all tasks with IDs greater than or equal to the target. The fix changed the condition to `WHERE id = ?`, ensuring only the target task is modified. This resolves the issue where editing one task would inadvertently rename later tasks.",
 "diff": "diff --git a/tracker/repository.py b/tracker/repository.py\nindex b82f358..8411052 100644\n--- a/tracker/repository.py\n+++ b/tracker/repository.py\n@@ -93,7 +93,7 @@ class TaskRepository:\n         args.append(now_iso)\n         args.append(task_id)\n         with self.db.transaction() as conn:\n-            cur = conn.execute(f\"UPDATE tasks SET {', '.join(assignments)} WHERE id >= ?\", args)\n+            cur = conn.execute(f\"UPDATE tasks SET {', '.join(assignments)} WHERE id = ?\", args)\n             if cur.rowcount == 0:\n                 raise NotFoundError(f\"task {task_id} not found\")\n         return self.get(task_id)\n",
